# In a loop the conversion of a number under a bit operator is an operation of its own, and the operator is built anew on what it gives. The other operand may be a boolean, which counts as 0 or 1:
# by that the form Numbers is chosen, and its code takes a number, undefined or null. So the boolean is converted in front as well. The walk stopped the build here without a word.
# RUN: walk f1 mechanisms=none+TypedOperations+UnboxedNumbers
# CHECK-LABEL: code f1:Body
# CHECK: = ToInt32
# CHECK: = ToNumber {{*}}form=Booleans
# CHECK: = BitAnd {{*}}form=Integers
# RUN(ALL): walk f1 mechanisms=all
# ALL-LABEL: code f1:Body
# ALL: = BitAnd {{*}}form=Integers
# a boolean or an integer, on the left
# RUN(EITHER): walk f2 mechanisms=none+TypedOperations+UnboxedNumbers
# EITHER-LABEL: code f2:Body
# EITHER: = ToNumber {{*}}form=NullableNumber
# EITHER: = BitOr {{*}}form=Numbers
# RUN(BOTH): walk f2 mechanisms=all
# BOTH-LABEL: code f2:Body
# BOTH: = BitOr {{*}}form=Numbers
# RUN(ANSWERS): run-in-shell
# ANSWERS: the source prints the same
